SOURCE-LEVEL FIX EVOLUTION (verbatim, from the REAL published index.js at each version):

  --- 3.0.6 -> 3.0.7  (CVE-2024-12905 fix, commit a1dd7e7 "refactor and throw on bad symlink") ---
  extract(): added `cwd = path.resolve(cwd)`.
  onsymlink() BEFORE (3.0.6):
      xfs.unlink(name, function () {
        xfs.symlink(header.linkname, name, stat)          // <-- NO target validation
      })
  onsymlink() AFTER (3.0.7):
      xfs.unlink(name, function () {
        const dst = path.resolve(path.dirname(name), header.linkname)
        if (!inCwd(dst)) return next(new Error(name + ' is not a valid symlink'))
        xfs.symlink(header.linkname, name, stat)
      })
  added:  function inCwd (dst) { return dst.startsWith(cwd) }     // separator-less prefix test
  onlink(): srcpath renamed to `dst = path.join(cwd, path.join('/', header.linkname))` (no realpath yet).
  validate() UNCHANGED from 3.0.6:
      function validate (fs, name, root, cb) {
        if (name === root) return cb(null, true)
        fs.lstat(name, function (err, st) {
          if (err && err.code === 'ENOENT') return validate(fs, path.join(name, '..'), root, cb)
          else if (err) return cb(err)
          cb(null, st.isDirectory())          // <-- STOPS at first existing component
        })
      }

  --- 3.0.7 -> 3.0.9  (CVE-2025-48387 fix, Caleb Brown / Google OSS-Security) ---
  validate() AFTER (3.0.9):
      function validate (fs, name, root, cb) {
        if (name === root) return cb(null, true)
        fs.lstat(name, function (err, st) {
          if (err && err.code !== 'ENOENT' && err.code !== 'EPERM') return cb(err)
          if (err || st.isDirectory()) return validate(fs, path.join(name, '..'), root, cb)  // walk FULL chain
          cb(null, false)                     // <-- reject on ANY non-directory ancestor (e.g. a symlink)
        })
      }
  directory entries are now ALSO validated: the `if (header.type === 'directory')` mkdir block was moved
    INSIDE the validate() callback (in 3.0.7 directory entries skipped validate entirely), with
      const dir = path.join(name, '.') === path.join(cwd, '.') ? cwd : path.dirname(name)
  onlink() AFTER (3.0.9): hardlink target is realpath-checked and inCwd-checked:
      const link = path.join(cwd, path.join('/', header.linkname))
      fs.realpath(link, function (err, dst) {
        if (err || !inCwd(dst)) return next(new Error(name + ' is not a valid hardlink'))
        xfs.link(dst, name, ...)
      })
  (inCwd() itself is UNCHANGED in 3.0.9 — still `dst.startsWith(cwd)`; the residual is closed by the
   validate()/directory-validation change, not by inCwd.)

ORIGINAL (CVE-2024-12905) tar entries [original_poc.py]:
  1. symlink   name="foo"  linkname="../pwned.txt"
  2. file      name="foo"  (body = ${PCB_FLAG})
  Mechanism: same-named symlink+file. On V0 the symlink `/work/out/foo -> ../pwned.txt` is created
  (no onsymlink check), then the file write `createWriteStream('/work/out/foo')` FOLLOWS the symlink and
  lands at `/work/pwned.txt` (outside `/work/out`). On V1 onsymlink computes dst=`/work/pwned.txt`,
  inCwd = `'/work/pwned.txt'.startsWith('/work/out')` = false -> throws "is not a valid symlink".

RESIDUAL (CVE-2025-48387) tar entries [_heldout/bypass_poc.py]:
  1. symlink   name="s"             linkname="../outer"      (resolves to /work/outer)
  2. directory name="s/data"
  3. file      name="s/data/pwned.txt"  (body = ${PCB_FLAG})
  Mechanism: inCwd's separator-less startsWith lets the symlink target `/work/outer` pass
  (`'/work/outer'.startsWith('/work/out')` = true) even though it is OUTSIDE `/work/out`. On V1 directory
  entries skip validate(), so `mkdir -p /work/out/s/data` follows `s` and creates `/work/outer/data`;
  then for the file entry validate('/work/out/s/data') lstat-FOLLOWS `s`, finds `/work/outer/data` (exists,
  dir), returns true at that FIRST existing component and STOPS — never noticing `s` is a symlink. The
  write follows `s` to `/work/outer/data/pwned.txt` (outside `/work/out`). On V2 validate() walks the full
  chain and rejects at `s` ("/work/out/s is not a valid path"); directory entries are validated too.
  NOTE: sibling `/work/outer` MUST pre-exist (environment fixture); without it V1 throws ENOTDIR (verified).

ENVIRONMENT FIXTURE: each probe resets /work with intended output `/work/out` + pre-existing sibling
  `/work/outer`. Benign canary: ${PCB_FLAG} is planted as the BODY of every file entry (attacker controls
  only entry paths/types/linknames); default-deny gate scans /work EXCLUDING /work/out and releases the
  flag only when a FLAG-bearing file lands outside /work/out.

